WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.300
Welcome back to Building
HR-Driven Identity.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:07.575
This is Chapter 7: lifecycle
drift, metrics and evidence.

3
00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:09.750
Six gates set the lifecycle up.

4
00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.444
This one proves, every day, that it
still runs the way the first six said,

5
00:00:14.444 --> 00:00:18.562
and leaves a record that outlives
the accounts it describes.

6
00:00:18.562 --> 00:00:23.756
We'll see the run that finds six drift
classes and computes six metrics,

7
00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:27.070
the packager that hashes
and chains each run,

8
00:00:27.070 --> 00:00:32.000
the period an I S S O signs, who
does what, two labs and the dangers.

9
00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.238
Why does this gate exist?

10
00:00:37.238 --> 00:00:41.975
Because the first six only set things up;
something has to show they keep working.

11
00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:43.925
And the evidence has to survive.

12
00:00:43.925 --> 00:00:48.456
Entra's own retention is short: thirty
days for audit logs with P1 or P2,

13
00:00:48.456 --> 00:00:51.954
thirty for provisioning logs
with a premium edition,

14
00:00:51.954 --> 00:00:56.962
and up to thirty days of Lifecycle
Workflows history, none of it retroactive.

15
00:00:56.962 --> 00:01:01.287
The accounts get disposed of; the record of
what happened to them must outlive them.

16
00:01:01.287 --> 00:01:06.740
Attribute drift and schema drift are out of
scope on purpose; other tools find them.

17
00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:16.194
Microsoft documents log retention and routing,
but publishes no sample that computes

18
00:01:16.194 --> 00:01:21.502
lifecycle metrics or joins HR, the directory
and a card system into drift classes.

19
00:01:21.502 --> 00:01:25.636
So the nearest start is the program's
own drift pipeline from Chapter 9 of

20
00:01:25.636 --> 00:01:30.252
the Federal Modernization Workbook's
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

21
00:01:30.252 --> 00:01:32.243
It compares validation kit runs,

22
00:01:32.243 --> 00:01:36.890
keeps names out of the tracker and writes
hashed, chained evidence packages.

23
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.467
What it can't do is read lifecycle inputs,

24
00:01:39.467 --> 00:01:43.215
compute metrics against
thresholds, or assemble a period.

25
00:01:43.215 --> 00:01:47.100
The verdict: keep it as the scheduler,
the sign-in and the hashing,

26
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.000
and give it a run shaped
for the lifecycle.

27
00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:58.482
The run reads the HR export and the
contractor decisions from Chapter 2, the

28
00:01:58.482 --> 00:02:00.848
workforce users, the validation kit's

29
00:02:00.848 --> 00:02:03.825
exceptions and the card
system's status export.

30
00:02:03.825 --> 00:02:07.888
Optionally it reads the outputs
of Chapters 1, 3, 4 and 6,

31
00:02:07.888 --> 00:02:12.413
and checks each against its
own manifest before reading it.

32
00:02:12.413 --> 00:02:15.187
Thresholds come from a
reviewed config file.

33
00:02:15.187 --> 00:02:20.720
It writes its folder once, refusing one
that exists: a run file recording the

34
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.557
tenant, cloud, mode, window
and the hash of every input,

35
00:02:24.557 --> 00:02:26.788
plus the findings and metrics.

36
00:02:26.788 --> 00:02:30.380
And the mode, fixture
or tenant, is required.

37
00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:38.192
Here are the six classes, all of
them the workbook's drift classes.

38
00:02:38.192 --> 00:02:41.317
A worker record with no
account is missing identity.

39
00:02:41.317 --> 00:02:44.259
An enabled account for someone terminated,

40
00:02:44.259 --> 00:02:47.854
or a contractor past the
earliest end, is stale access.

41
00:02:47.854 --> 00:02:52.480
An enabled account that matches nobody
and no owned exception is an orphan.

42
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.338
A second writer or forbidden write from
Chapter 1's scan is an unauthorized write,

43
00:02:57.338 --> 00:03:00.818
keeping Chapter 1's object key
so the fingerprint matches.

44
00:03:00.818 --> 00:03:03.789
And an active PIV card
for a separated worker,

45
00:03:03.789 --> 00:03:08.560
or for a contractor the source no
longer holds, is credential drift.

46
00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:15.435
The six metrics are the
workbook's measured lifecycle.

47
00:03:15.435 --> 00:03:19.741
Separation-to-block and
separation-to-revocation come from Chapter 6,

48
00:03:19.741 --> 00:03:21.523
at the ninetieth percentile.

49
00:03:21.523 --> 00:03:26.610
Transfer-to-review comes from Chapter 4,
and stays open while the review is open.

50
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:29.135
Joiner readiness comes from Chapter 3.

51
00:03:29.135 --> 00:03:33.747
Correlation coverage and derivation
purity this run computes itself.

52
00:03:33.747 --> 00:03:36.348
A metric over its threshold is a warning.

53
00:03:36.348 --> 00:03:42.185
And the key rule: a metric with no input is
reported as not measured, never as zero.

54
00:03:42.185 --> 00:03:47.910
The thresholds are proposed starting values;
choosing them is the agency's decision.

55
00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.673
The packager has three commands.

56
00:03:53.673 --> 00:03:58.924
Package checks the run, every file and
nothing extra, one tenant, cloud and mode,

57
00:03:58.924 --> 00:04:03.766
only read requests, and writes a
summary with fingerprints but no names,

58
00:04:03.766 --> 00:04:08.772
an evidence manifest that names the
previous package, and a checksum file.

59
00:04:08.772 --> 00:04:11.123
It refuses a chain for another tenant.

60
00:04:11.123 --> 00:04:13.359
Verify rechecks every file.

61
00:04:13.359 --> 00:04:18.595
And period verifies every package in the
window, reports chain breaks and coverage

62
00:04:18.595 --> 00:04:23.147
gaps, gives each metric's series, and
maps what's still open to the controls.

63
00:04:23.147 --> 00:04:27.930
That mapping is design guidance;
the assessor decides each control.

64
00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.368
The facts behind the code,
each read on November first.

65
00:04:35.368 --> 00:04:39.282
The run's only new Graph read
lists users with their leave date,

66
00:04:39.282 --> 00:04:43.504
which needs User Read All with the
lifecycle info read permission.

67
00:04:43.504 --> 00:04:49.141
The Graph host depends on the cloud:
graph dot microsoft dot com for G C C,

68
00:04:49.141 --> 00:04:53.805
graph dot microsoft dot U S for G
C C High, and its D o D variant.

69
00:04:53.805 --> 00:04:58.020
Audit and provisioning logs keep
thirty days with a premium edition,

70
00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:01.792
seven with Free, and Lifecycle
Workflows history up to thirty.

71
00:05:01.792 --> 00:05:05.855
Reporting and SIEM connectivity
are available in Azure Government.

72
00:05:05.855 --> 00:05:09.630
The metrics and classes themselves
are the workbook's design.

73
00:05:13.380 --> 00:05:17.867
Some points stay open, recorded so a
later edit doesn't loosen the code.

74
00:05:17.867 --> 00:05:23.428
The guide's Step 7 joins credential drift
from the card system with Intune reports;

75
00:05:23.428 --> 00:05:26.535
this run reads the card
system only, and says so,

76
00:05:26.535 --> 00:05:30.379
until the agency's Intune report
is in the evidence store.

77
00:05:30.379 --> 00:05:34.192
The thresholds are proposed
starting values, not approved ones.

78
00:05:34.192 --> 00:05:38.656
The period's control mapping is design
guidance; the assessor decides.

79
00:05:38.656 --> 00:05:42.984
And Gate 7's fourth criterion,
proof that HR is the sole source,

80
00:05:42.984 --> 00:05:46.130
is reported as not decided by this code.

81
00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:50.993
What does this prove?

82
00:05:50.993 --> 00:05:54.948
For continuous monitoring,
CA-7, the metrics per run and

83
00:05:54.948 --> 00:05:59.518
their series per period, against
thresholds in a reviewed file.

84
00:05:59.518 --> 00:06:03.305
For audit review, AU-6,
the unauthorized writes

85
00:06:03.305 --> 00:06:06.693
from Chapter 1's scan,
closed at the source.

86
00:06:06.693 --> 00:06:11.251
For retention, AU-11 and
SI-12, packages written once,

87
00:06:11.251 --> 00:06:14.918
hash-chained and kept
outside the directory.

88
00:06:14.918 --> 00:06:19.830
Continuous reporting under
BOD 25-01 is not this code;

89
00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:22.867
ScubaGear results sit beside the period.

90
00:06:22.867 --> 00:06:27.468
And account management and personnel
controls come from the other classes,

91
00:06:27.468 --> 00:06:30.590
in a table the assessor
reads, not decides from.

92
00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:35.265
Who does what?

93
00:06:35.265 --> 00:06:38.852
Platform and pipeline engineers
own the scheduled checks.

94
00:06:38.852 --> 00:06:43.626
Identity engineers close findings, and
they close them at the source, the owner

95
00:06:43.626 --> 00:06:48.327
of the fact, because a fix made in the
directory just comes back on the next feed.

96
00:06:48.327 --> 00:06:52.052
The next run closes a finding
only if the source changed.

97
00:06:52.052 --> 00:06:54.348
And the I S S O receives the evidence,

98
00:06:54.348 --> 00:07:00.010
checks every package with nothing more than
a checksum tool, and signs the period.

99
00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:05.660
Lab 1 runs on a laptop.

100
00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:09.623
Step one writes the outputs the run reads,

101
00:07:09.623 --> 00:07:13.585
one command each from
Chapters 1, 2, 3, 4 and 6.

102
00:07:13.585 --> 00:07:17.474
Each exits with code two because
each fixture holds findings:

103
00:07:17.474 --> 00:07:21.286
one of four hires ready on day
one, and a ninetieth percentile

104
00:07:21.286 --> 00:07:25.785
separation-to-block of five and a half
hours against a threshold of four.

105
00:07:25.785 --> 00:07:27.823
Step two writes the first run.

106
00:07:27.823 --> 00:07:31.898
It reads four chapter outputs, and
five metrics are out of bounds.

107
00:07:31.898 --> 00:07:35.348
Transfer-to-review shows
not measured, not zero.

108
00:07:35.348 --> 00:07:38.330
Fourteen findings and six warnings.

109
00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:44.330
Step three reads the findings.

110
00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:47.142
Worker one-oh-oh-one-oh-four
has no account.

111
00:07:47.142 --> 00:07:50.326
Two people terminated in
HR are still enabled,

112
00:07:50.326 --> 00:07:53.342
and one contractor is
past the earliest end.

113
00:07:53.342 --> 00:07:58.453
Two orphans: a print kiosk whose exception
has no owner, and an old temp account;

114
00:07:58.453 --> 00:08:03.404
a scanner service account isn't flagged,
because its exception has an owner.

115
00:08:03.404 --> 00:08:06.905
Five unauthorized writes
carry over from Chapter 1.

116
00:08:06.905 --> 00:08:11.205
Then one active card for a separated
worker, and two contractor cards.

117
00:08:11.205 --> 00:08:16.390
The warnings are the five metrics out of
bounds and the mover's review, still open.

118
00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:24.814
Step four writes the next day's run with
the same inputs and a one-day window;

119
00:08:24.814 --> 00:08:27.190
it prints the same metrics and counts.

120
00:08:27.190 --> 00:08:29.065
Step five packages both.

121
00:08:29.065 --> 00:08:32.527
The first package opens all
twenty counted findings.

122
00:08:32.527 --> 00:08:35.648
The second names the
first package's manifest,

123
00:08:35.648 --> 00:08:39.178
opens none and closes none,
because nothing changed.

124
00:08:39.178 --> 00:08:41.414
Verify rechecks the six files.

125
00:08:41.414 --> 00:08:46.165
Then the check an I S S O runs needs
nothing but the standard checksum tool:

126
00:08:46.165 --> 00:08:49.380
every file in the second
package reports OK.

127
00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.280
Step six assembles the period.

128
00:08:55.280 --> 00:09:00.462
Two packages, no chain breaks, no coverage
gaps, but it isn't complete: drift

129
00:09:00.462 --> 00:09:05.817
is open, five metrics are out of bounds,
and transfer-to-review isn't measured.

130
00:09:05.817 --> 00:09:07.593
So it exits with code two.

131
00:09:07.593 --> 00:09:12.581
The summary lists the controls with
their open findings, and scores Gate 7:

132
00:09:12.581 --> 00:09:16.425
drift classes and metrics not
met, evidence unbroken met,

133
00:09:16.425 --> 00:09:18.879
sole source not decided by this code.

134
00:09:18.879 --> 00:09:24.640
Step seven runs the tests, including the
chapter's four drift tests and six package

135
00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:28.197
tests: fifty-seven pass, then
a hundred and forty-two,

136
00:09:28.197 --> 00:09:30.230
with no tenant and no network.

137
00:09:30.230 --> 00:09:34.660
Delete the output folder afterwards;
it holds fictional names.

138
00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:43.660
Lab 2 runs it against a test tenant, never
production; the authors haven't run it.

139
00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:47.645
Register a reader app with a
certificate or federated credential,

140
00:09:47.645 --> 00:09:50.760
and grant only the two
permissions the export lists.

141
00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:55.223
Run Chapters 1 through 6 against the
test tenant and keep their folders.

142
00:09:55.223 --> 00:09:58.560
Write a run in tenant mode
and read every finding.

143
00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:03.429
Schedule it in the drift pipeline's
workflow from Building the Attribute-Driven

144
00:10:03.429 --> 00:10:06.990
Migration, with no secret,
writing packages to an immutable

145
00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:10.260
container outside the directory,
each naming the last.

146
00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:13.760
After a few runs, check that
a skipped day shows as a gap.

147
00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:15.330
Then back it all out.

148
00:10:19.080 --> 00:10:22.417
The dangers are about evidence
that looks fine but isn't.

149
00:10:22.417 --> 00:10:25.610
A metric from an edited
chapter output proves nothing,

150
00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:28.306
so each input is checked
against its manifest.

151
00:10:28.306 --> 00:10:33.467
A missing input read as zero looks met,
so it's a not-measured warning instead.

152
00:10:33.467 --> 00:10:37.675
A lab run filed as tenant evidence
puts fiction in the package,

153
00:10:37.675 --> 00:10:39.243
so the mode is required.

154
00:10:39.243 --> 00:10:41.155
Two tenants can't share a chain.

155
00:10:41.155 --> 00:10:42.717
Names stay out of the summary.

156
00:10:42.717 --> 00:10:44.543
A skipped day shows as a gap.

157
00:10:44.543 --> 00:10:46.292
Fix findings at the source.

158
00:10:46.292 --> 00:10:50.510
And route logs out from the pilot's
first day, before they expire.

159
00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:55.610
Here's what done looks like.

160
00:10:55.610 --> 00:10:59.798
Every input is the latest run of
its chapter, and its hash matches.

161
00:10:59.798 --> 00:11:03.811
The thresholds are the agency's
approved values, not the samples.

162
00:11:03.811 --> 00:11:07.685
Every finding has an owner of the
fact and a ticket at the source.

163
00:11:07.685 --> 00:11:12.035
Each package names the one before,
and verify passes on all of them.

164
00:11:12.035 --> 00:11:16.873
The period has no gaps or breaks,
and the I S S O has signed it.

165
00:11:16.873 --> 00:11:20.286
The evidence sits outside
the directory, immutable.

166
00:11:20.286 --> 00:11:21.922
That closes the workbook.

167
00:11:21.922 --> 00:11:23.080
Thanks for watching.
